  "thought": "I need to examine types, quest examples, economy constraints, and validators beyond the schema.\n\nI will call registry_query before designing the quest. I also need to check related types and any nearby example quests.\n\nI am reviewing the game mechanics and narrative context. The quest must feature the Payphone job router and heat systems. It should avoid cars and souls. The plot involves Don Fax hiring Rivet for sabotage at guarded dock storage. New characters include a nurse, gunsmith, and scientist. Rivet survived a factory accident and disassembled the police. The opening monologue transitions to a Payphone call from Don Fax.\n\nDead voices may be heard but not understood. General Krump appears on the radio.\n\nEconomy constraints are strict: cash 500–2500, heat ceiling 3, max 60 enemies across 6 waves, and 6–14 minutes. The owner forbids timers. The quest must run 12–22 lines with at least three beats and at most four new characters.\n\nThe nurse, gunsmith, and scientist are existing cast entries with fixed IDs. Their names and personalities are invented.\n\nThe nurse, gunsmith, and scientist are existing cast entries. Their status should be \"existing\" rather than \"new\", so design notes are optional. I will still invent fresh names for them.\n\nThe brief forbids reusing any personality, voice, or look from earlier drafts. The nurse, gunsmith, and scientist IDs are fixed, but their display names must be invented from scratch.\n\nI am weighing whether to mark the nurse, gunsmith, and scientist as \"new\" characters. That would force a one-line designNote for their look, which supports the brief's requirement to invent fresh appearances. Their current IDs are just positions, not proper names.\n\nI will mark the nurse, gunsmith, and scientist as existing characters and include design notes for their looks anyway. I am also checking whether General Krump, MC Crumbtray, or Patch should count as existing characters to stay within the four-new-character limit.Checking types.ts for QuestProposal validation and game context for dock and factory.\n\nChecking for validators on economy bands and line counts. The brief is detailed enough to proceed. I will look for extra type fields, sample quests, and registry conventions before finalizing the quest.\n\nThe mandatory registry_query reminder may delay the JSON-only reply. I will query for quest design patterns and check types.ts for the claimsBeat pattern.\n\nThe beat naming regex accepts `act1.soul-1`. I cannot claim this beat because the quest forbids unlocking souls and requires the scientist's first quest to finish first. Story state variables must use the `q_<name>` snake_case format.\n\nI will name the quest state `q_fax_first_job_done` or `q_dock_sabotage_done`. Reads should be empty because this is the first quest and no prior state exists to read.The registry did not contain the game-development domain.\n\nA GTA-style narrative quest is being designed. Titles like \"PER MY LAST FAX\" and \"Paper Jam\" are under consideration. The owner sample references faxing to kill the inventor of email.\n\nThe fax-machine bureaucratic tone is a game pitch, not a locked personality. Earlier voice bibles are withheld for this run, so each speaker will have a distinct voice.\n\nThe fax name is kept, but the paper-jam gag is discarded. A fresh personality will be invented for Don Fax.\n\nDon Fax is being shaped as a soft-spoken funeral director of crime. He speaks in dry, bureaucratic cadences like a hold-message and confirmation number. Murder is treated as routine shipping manifest work. He never raises his voice and apologizes for violence like a clerk for a fee.\n\nRivet will be extremely nervous and unstable. His first lines are a monologue to himself before the payphone rings.\n\nRivet survived a factory line accident and disassembled the police. The voices of the dead are a symptom, not a power.\n\nA robot nurse named June Clamp or Nia Suture will heal Rivet. This establishes a love-interest hook without launching a romance questline.\n\nThe nurse is now Willa Gage. She measures damage like weather and flirts by diagnosing him as \"still worth the scrap.\" She keeps a drawer of \"parts that used to be people\" and writes her frequency on his chest plate in marker.\n\nThe gunsmith is Rook Tanzer. He gives the first weapon and becomes a buddy through personal requests later.\n\nRook Tanzer's first gun is ugly and broken. He mutters that the weapon owes him a favor, not Rivet. The gun lacks its nameplate; Rook says the army stamped it and wants it back someday. This seeds a personal request without starting it.\n\nDr. Vesper Quoin is the scientist. She hears the static and gets delighted and scared. She asks Rivet to return when the whispers learn verbs. She does not teach him voices yet. She does not unlock the gearbox.\n\nKrump is a human antagonist on the radio during the dock fight. He broadcasts as a cheerfully bloodless halftime sponsor, calling the dock project heritage steel and protesters unlicensed emotions.\n\nMC Crumbtray is a diegetic DJ who reacts live against Krump. The radio war will be kept minimal to save line budget.\n\nPatch is the payphone job router. It speaks as the phone itself, giving a mechanic tutorial line about not apologizing to machines.\n\nThe quest structure will have four or five beats. The first is Static: Rivet hears unparseable dead voices outside the factory, the payphone rings, and Don Fax hires him.\n\nThe second beat is The Office. Rivet meets the nurse, gunsmith, and scientist at Don Fax's underground place. The city's heat is explained as the cops already being mad at him. The sabotage target is a guarded dock storage facility for an army project.\n\nThe third beat is The Receipt. Rivet travels on foot to the dock. Payphone heat rises in waves of weak human soldiers. Krump broadcasts live from the radio.\n\nThe fourth beat is Cover Sheet. Rivet returns or calls back. Don Fax pays. Krump promises more. The scientist's hook lingers.\n\nThe job pays 1100 cash. Heat peaks at 3. Enemy count is 48 to 54. There are 5 to 6 waves. The mission lasts 6 to 14 minutes.\n\nThe quest length is fixed at 12 minutes. There is no time limit.\n\nFail states include Rivet being destroyed, aborting the sabotage, and dying to waves.\n\nHeat mechanics are defined: killing humans or being seen raises heat, which escalates enemy waves. The ceiling remains 3.\n\nThe payphone job router will connect future jobs the same way.\n\nHeat decays when Rivet hides in the stacks or breaks line of sight. No souls or car mechanics are introduced. The fingerprint field will list \"payphone-job-router\" as the headline mechanic.\n\nThe fingerprint will list \"payphone-and-heat\" as the headline mechanic, since both systems matter. The location is guarded dock storage at an army warehouse. The payoff kind may combine cash with the first weapon.\n\nThe payoff kind is set to \"contract-cash\". The gunsmith gives the first weapon mid-quest; Don Fax pays the cash. The role enum is being checked for Rivet's protagonist status.\n\nRivet must be listed in the characters array because every line speaker needs an ID. The role enum lacks a protagonist slot, so \"ally\" is being considered as the closest fit for the player character.\n\nThe schema lacks a protagonist role. \"Ally\" and \"bystander\" were rejected as semantically wrong. The workspace contains no type definitions to resolve the mismatch.lineGuard raises a NO_VOICE_BIBLE error for existing speakers when no voice bible is present.NO_VOICE_BIBLE is an error for speakers who are not new characters and lack a voice bible.\n\nThe brief says earlier voice bibles are withheld for this run. Existing characters will fail the NO_VOICE_BIBLE guard unless marked new or voice bible files exist. The guard only checks file existence, not content.Voice bibles exist for rivet, don-fax, general-krump, mc-crumbtray, and patch.\n\nVoice bibles are absent for nurse, gunsmith, and scientist. The brief forbids reusing earlier traits or voices. I will invent fresh voices for these characters.\n\nThe three supporters will trigger NO_VOICE_BIBLE errors because their voice bibles are absent. The existing characters rivet and don-fax will not error since their bibles exist.\n\nThe supporters will speak despite the missing voice bibles. The owner intentionally withheld those files, so the resulting warnings are expected. Removing their lines would fail the introduction requirement.\n\nI will not read any existing voice bibles to avoid copying. I will invent fresh voices for all characters, including Krump, within the brief's constraints. I will keep new character additions minimal and use project targets when possible. I am checking the cast names now.\n\nI will assign proper names to the three supporters rather than using their positions. I am weighing multiple quest flags against a single completion flag for the act.\n\nI will write quest flags so later sessions can read them. I will not read soul_gearbox or claim act1.soul-1. The gun is a weapon, not a soul, so reward.unlocks should not include soul:gearbox. A weapon unlock string is acceptable. The gunsmith giving the gun is quest content, not a new mechanic.\n\nThe reward will include a weapon unlock such as `weapon:loaner-scatter` so the gunsmith's gift is mechanically real. Rivet arrives at the dock with heat 1 from his earlier factory destruction, and the army response scales up to peak 3. Patch or Don Fax will explain the heat mechanic by describing it as a number the city keeps.\n\nThe heat explanation will note that two is already a warning and three is when the boats notice. The dock encounter will use six waves of weak enemies totaling about forty-eight to fifty-four units. Play time is estimated at twelve minutes with no timer. Fail states are Rivet destroyed or the dock project remaining intact.\n\nA new fail state is added: killing the three supporters at Don's place fails the contract and causes Don Fax to hang up permanently. The act will have four beats. The first is a busy-signal monologue outside the factory where Patch connects and Don Fax hires Rivet. The second is the back room where the nurse heals him, the gunsmith gives him a weapon, the scientist notes the head-noise, and the dock job is briefly introduced.\n\nThe third beat is a foot march to the guarded dock storage. Sabotage the army project while waves of weak humans attack. Krump will speak on the radio and Crumbtray will talk back. Heat climbs to three. The fourth beat is cash payment at the phone or office. Don Fax pays and Krump signs off promising the city will be cleaned. The draft will run sixteen to eighteen lines. Rivet's voice is mad, extremely nervous, unsure, and unstable.\n\nRivet's voice is fragmented. He restarts sentences, second-guesses himself, counts fingers, and apologizes to objects. He uses first person because he has not yet joined the Choir. His madness is raw and unfiltered; he flinches at his own hands and swears in a terrified way.\n\nDon Fax is a calm night-shift harbormaster of crime. He speaks like stamping a bill of lading. He calls Rivet \"the survivor\" rather than a friend. He never shouts.\n\nDon Fax swears only when a number is wrong. He uses short sentences and counts. The nurse is Willa Gage. A designNote will be added to her entry for the art pass.\n\nWilla Gage is a squat medic-robot in a stained white apron. Her face is a round analog gauge. She holds one arm as a pressure cuff and the other with an unlit cigarette. She speaks like a weather report about bodies. She heals by insulting the damage. A callback frequency for the next leak is written for her own purposes. This plants a love hook without starting a date. The gunsmith will be named Bram Pell.\n\nBram Pell is a lanky robot in a leather welder's skirt with three arms and safety glasses welded to his face. He speaks to the gun rather than to Rivet, grudgingly and superstitiously about weapons. He calls the loaner-scatter a \"loaner with a grudge\" and seeds a personal hook: the gun's last owner was his brother-unit who went to the docks and never returned. He does not ask Rivet to find him now.\n\nDr. Ivo Quench will be the scientist.\n\nDr. Ivo Quench is tall with a coat made of cable ties. His head is a cracked oscilloscope with one wandering green line and his pockets hold labeled jars of static. He speaks delightedly but rudely and faster when he hears the whispers. He wants to know what is wrong with Rivet's head but never explains it or teaches control. He never mentions souls or the gearbox. He plants a hook by telling Rivet to return when the noise learns a verb. He cannot translate the whisper either. It remains a symptom rather than a power.\n\nGeneral Krump will be a fresh voice as a cheerful procurement officer of death.\n\nGeneral Krump speaks in sponsor-reads and unit citations. He never swears. He calls the dock project a \"readiness cradle.\" His jokes mock military power. They are aimed at the army's self-image.\n\nMC Crumbtray will be a diegetic DJ on the dock radio. He speaks like a hungry forklift auctioneer. He mocks Krump without knowing Rivet's name.\n\nPatch will appear as a single line on the phone.\n\nPatch will speak in a mechanical, slightly judgmental tone. He tells Rivet he must walk because he has no car and this is not a metaphor. The draft will maximize profanity and mad lines while keeping brutality broad and jokes aimed at power. Editorial tells such as \"it's not X, it's Y\" or \"why? because\" will be avoided. The total line count will stay between twelve and twenty-two. Every line must either belong to a beat's lineIds or carry a when tag. Heat barks can use both beats and when tags.\n\nI am drafting the first beat's lineIds. Rivet's monologue and Patch's phone line are assigned. Heat barks will use when tags to avoid warnings without being listed in beats.\n\nThe first beat now has four lines: Patch connects, Don Fax hires Rivet, Rivet accepts with an unstable yes, and Don explains the heat mechanic. The second beat holds Willa's healing and hook, Bram's gun and hook, Ivo's head observation and hook, and Don's brief. The third beat covers Krump on the radio, Crumbtray on the radio, Rivet at the dock, and a wave update. The fourth beat holds Don's payment, Rivet's after-dock line, and Krump's sign-off. The draft totals twenty lines; a heat bark with a when tag may push it to twenty-one. A dead voice that Rivet cannot understand is also being considered.\n\nThe dead voices will be unidentifiable gibberish in Rivet's head, not a named speaker. The draft will avoid giving them a soul-1 identity. Krump appearing on the radio is treated as the owner beat for first robot bosses hiring Rivet. This quest handles the hire and the radio appearance without claiming soul-1.\n\nThe location is named The Carbon Sink, a basement beneath a closed customs scale-house. Krump will only appear on the radio; he does not hire Rivet.\n\nThe dock is Berth 9 Cold Storage. The army project is Project STILL, a prototype compliance cradle that plays a tone to make robots kneel. Sabotage will jam the tone-core so it screams instead, or cut the cradle and dump it in the slip.\n\nThe owner will hint that the dock storage is tied to the humans and the army, but the deeper twist about US military involvement in the souls conspiracy is saved for act three. Krump can appear as a public army figure on the radio without revealing that he caused Rivet's accident or the voices. A fax-bomb disguised as a shipping seal is being considered as the sabotage verb.\n\nThe sabotage objective is to destroy the Project STILL cradle at Berth 9 Cold Storage. It will use a hold-interact that cannot fail from a timer. The player must weld the crane so the crate drops into the water. Leaving the yard or dying ends the mission. Waves continue until the cradle is destroyed, capped at six waves.\n\nHeat levels will scale wave difficulty. Level one sends dock security. Level two sends army clerks with pistols. Level three brings a boat spotlight and more bodies. All waves use weak one-hit kills. Attrition is the challenge.\n\nDon will not name the Gearbox as unlockable content. He will say no car is drivable and that souls must not be used or unlocked. A line about Rivet touching a door will show he cannot enter cars.\n\nA payphone will ring near the factory exit to announce the first job. Patch will announce the line and answer it to start the contract. Later jobs will ring the nearest phone, teaching that phones are the router rather than a one-off cutscene. A second ring at the end may confirm payment.\n\nRivet already carries heat from the factory police kill. The HUD will show it as stars. Shooting army units in the open raises heat. Breaking line of sight behind containers drops it slowly. At level three a searchlight boat pins the player and waves thicken. The ceiling is three and the peak is three. Don, Patch, and a bark will each explain the mechanic in one line. Two new mechanics are being added to the array: payphone-job-router and heat.\n\nThe uniqueness guard compares giver, verb, and newMechanic. The fingerprint field can only hold one string. I will list both mechanics in the array. I am deciding whether to put \"payphone-job-router\" or \"heat\" in the fingerprint.\n\nThe fingerprint will hold \"payphone-job-router\" because the phone call is the quest's spine. Heat is exercised inside it. Both mechanics are required, but the fingerprint only allows one entry.\n\nBoth mechanics are game systems, not proof-of-fun tools, so their home field is set to \"game\".\n\nRivet is exempt from the characters array when he does not speak. The brief requires adding him whenever he does.The brief specifies variant A only. Variant A matches the act's expected tone.\n\nNurse, gunsmith, and scientist are allies in this quest, not givers. Only R.Fax is the giver. Rivet is also an ally, since there is no protagonist.\n\nGeneral Krump is the antagonist. Mc-Crumbtray and Patch are voices. The council totals eight characters, all existing. Proper names will go in the `name` field.\n\nDraft proper names are Willa Gage, Bram Pell, and Dr. Ivo Quench. The writerly style feels off against the cast's punchy GTA names. Salve Mint is a possible alternative for the nurse.\n\nThe final names are June Clamp for the nurse, Ned Rasp for the gunsmith, and Dr. Lumen Peck for the scientist.\n\nDesign notes will be added only for the three supports. Existing characters keep their names and positions, but their looks, voices, and personalities must not be reused from earlier drafts.\n\nThe brief requires inventing every character fresh, including existing ones. Design notes must be added to all council members, not just the three supports.\n\nRivet's look will remain as-is. The factory intro already established his silhouette, so inventing a fresh one would contradict that canon. Design notes will be reserved for the three supports and other council members.\n\nDon Fax will be reimagined as a heavy robot in a customs officer's greatcoat, stamp-hand for a right fist, and visor like a green bill-of-lading screen. He will always hold an unread clipboard. This avoids the old paper-jam fax machine cliché while keeping the name. Krump is human.\n\nGeneral Krump will be a small man on a big radio, in a dress uniform too tight for his frame. His medals are office-supply pins. He smiles like a man selling war insurance. The top-down cutout shows a tiny human with a huge hat and a radio mic the size of his torso.\n\nDesign notes will be added for the three supports, Don Fax, and Krump. Existing characters keep their looks.\n\nCrumbtray and Patch will keep their existing UI looks. Patch is a payphone. Design notes are optional for them.\n\nThe three supports are existing characters. This keeps the council under the four-new-character cap.\n\nLines must be mad, profane, and aimed at army bosses. I will draft them now and check the editorial regex.\n\nOnly one quest flag will be set: `q_berth9_sabotaged` to mark the intro quest complete. No separate flags for meeting the council or other side quests are needed.\n\nThe player starts with 1600 cash, reflecting a first-time hitman on an army dock job.\n\nThe quest unlocks Ned Rasp's loaner gun. Fail states include Rivet being destroyed, leaving Berth 9 with the cradle intact, or any support dying. Enemy waves total fifty-four weak human units across six waves. Heat peaks at three. The time limit is twelve minutes. Objectives are answering the payphone, meeting Don Fax's people, sabotaging the still cradle at Berth 9, and returning to collect payment.\n\nThe quest will not use a durationS timer. Heat begins at one from the factory cops and rises automatically with each wave as the army commits, peaking at three; it decays slightly between waves when the player hides behind containers. The Krump radio plays during wave three, and Crumbtray answers on that same radio during wave four. Rivet's monologue must precede the payphone ring.\n\nDrafting lines now. Rivet's fingers line is a mad, funny monologue about hands and cops. His static line is a paranoid, character-driven speech about someone talking in his teeth. Patch's ring line is a dry, funny progression piece about answering the phone and walking away to avoid worse robots.\n\nDrafted Don Fax's hire line. It mocks his sloppy invoice and demands he ruin an army toy at Berth Nine for cash, with no apologies to corpses. Added Rivet's hit line. He claims he does hits now and complains about the morning bolts before demanding a destination. Added Don Fax's heat line. It warns that little stars are heat and the city is already mad about the cops.\n\nDrafted Don Fax's heat line. It warns that heat rises when the army watches and drops if you hide behind a crate, with a threat about not collecting four stars and paperwork.\n\nDrafted June Clamp's heal line. She complains that the player is leaking from a place that is not a place, then threatens to staple the afternoon back in and charge for fainting.\n\nDrafted June Clamp's hook line. She claims the staple is medicine and wrote her frequency on it in case another hole springs.\n\nDrafted June Clamp's final line. She threatens to unstaple the player if they call her medicine a love letter.\n\nDrafted Ned Rasp's gun line. He calls the weapon ugly because the player is new, says Loaner hates heroes, and frames the player as an errand.\n\nDrafted Ned Rasp's hook line. He tells the player to bring Loaner home in one piece so he can reveal his dumb name and maybe let them sit.\n\nDrafted Dr. Lumen Peck's head line. He warns the player's skull is broadcasting and losing, and tells them not to nod because that looks like consent.\n\nDrafted his hook line. He says he will not fix the player today, wants to know why dead people are using their mouth as a waiting room, and tells them to come back when one of them manages a whole verb.\n\nDrafted Don Fax's brief line. It describes Berth Nine as cold storage where the army is bolting a machine called Project STILL, warns that soldiers die if the player frowns at them, and mocks clocks.\n\nDrafted General Krump's radio line. It opens with \"This is General Krump, live from a chair that cost more than your block.\"\n\nDrafted General Krump's radio line. It frames Berth Nine as a readiness cradle and warns that a rogue appliance touching the tone is having a mood outside the budget.\n\nDrafted Crumbtray's cut-in line. He interrupts Krump's nap to tell the caller on the dock to ask for louder action, mocking Krump's men for shooting like they fear the union.\n\nDrafted Rivet's pins line. It opens with \"Pin one. Pin two.\"\n\nDrafted Rivet's pins line. It is a mad, violent monologue about pinning the still cradle and a final goodbye to a horrible flute.\n\nDrafted a heat-bark for Rivet. When heat reaches two he hides behind a box of someone else's war and complains that the box is judging him.\n\nDrafted Don Fax's pay line. He tells the player that tone-core is in the slip and Berth Nine is a puddle with a budget. He offers eleven hundred cash as insulting but agreed.\n\nDon Fax's pay line now offers eighteen hundred cash, with a line about rounding down because the player apologized to a forklift. The reward is set to 1800. Drafting Rivet's final line next.\n\nDrafted Rivet's final line. It is a mad, violent monologue about walking away with a gun and a staple and a head full of silent people.\n\nDrafted General Krump's close line. It mocks the cradle as an unscheduled swim and threatens smiles paid in artillery.\n\nThe total now stands at twenty lines including the heat bark.\n\nThe heat bark is assigned to beat 3 with a when tag. The twenty-line count fits the 12–22 limit. I am scanning the drafted lines for the \"it's not X, it's Y\" pattern and found none so far.\n\nThe self-answered question regex found no matches. The closing-aphorism check also passed, since \"we are\" does not trigger the forbidden \"we all\" pattern. The drafted lines avoid both editorial traps.\n\nDon Fax's voice stays consistent as a calm, paperwork-hating clerk. The line \"Beautiful work\" is kept as an appraisal rather than friendship. He remains a neutral payer who does not report the player to anyone. King, Servo, Torque, and Kronk are not placed in the quest. Gearbox is not named as an unlock; the scientist simply says cars do not open for him.\n\nThe profanity toggle is not required. The tone will be maximally profane without listing every curse. June Clamp will add a \"damn\" to her line about charging for fainting. Ned Rasp will use \"shit.\" Krump stays clean to contrast with his power.\n\nThe drafted lines avoid punching down at civilians. The critique targets power directly: Krump’s small guns and large purpose, the army’s fear of the union, and the government’s trust in clocks. Rivet’s madness is strictly personal; he uses “I” rather than “we,” keeping him separate from the Choir. This quest opens Act One and continues the factory story.\n\nThe opening is set outside the factory after the accident and the cops. Each character leaves a hook for future quests: Don Fax offers more jobs, the Scientist invites a return, June Clamp drops her frequency, Ned Rasp names his brother later, and Krump notices the player. No questlines are started yet. The draft passes the whyNotFiller test by delivering a specific laugh, a reveal, and a turn.\n\nRivet's finger-counting monologue frames his madness as a clipable joke. Don Fax teaches the payphone and heat stars during the same call that hires him. June, Ned, and Lumen each leave a hook without opening their quests. Project STILL entering the slip is the turn: Krump learns an appliance did it.\n\nThe logline is drafted. It covers the payphone hire, the basement meeting, and the cold-storage walk into Project STILL.\n\nA fresh title is needed. Candidates include Berth Nine, Project STILL, The Tone Goes In The Water, and Loaner.\n\nThe title is now \"Unscheduled Swim.\" It mocks Krump's readiness cradle and the army's mood budget.\n\nThe title is now \"Four Pins.\" The logline is tightened to cover the payphone hire, the nurse stapling, the gunsmith loan, and the scientist's refusal.\n\nThe fingerprint is now set. It marks Don Fax as the giver, sabotage as the core verb, and the payphone job router as the new mechanic. The location is Berth Nine cold storage and the payoff kind is first contract. Heat is a second mechanic because the fingerprint slot is full.\n\nThe fingerprint mechanic is now fixed as \"payphone-job-router\" alone, with heat kept as a separate entry. Next up are the one-line design notes for Don Fax and the three supporting characters.\n\nThe design notes are now drafted. The nurse is a squat medic-robot with a blood-pressure cuff arm and a staple gun on her hip. The gunsmith is lanky and three-armed in a welder skirt with safety glasses welded to his skull. The scientist is tall with a coat of cable ties, a cracked oscilloscope head, and grease-pencil jars in his pockets. Don Fax is broad with a customs greatcoat, a rubber stamp hand, and an unread bill-of-lading visor.\n\nDon Fax's look is deliberately fresh, avoiding the fax-machine cliché. A design note for General Krump is added for the radio portrait, though he may not appear as a cutout this quest. Design notes are finalized for the nurse, gunsmith, scientist, and Don Fax; Rivet and the phone prop are excluded.\n\nCrumbtray is skipped because he has no portrait. Rivet is tagged as an ally, not a voice. Only Don Fax will be the mission giver.\n\nThe four beats are now mapped to verbs, targets, and locations. Beat one is answering the payphone at the factory gate. Beat two is meeting June Clamp, Ned Rasp, and Dr. Lumen Peck at the Carbon Sink. Beat three is sabotaging Project STILL tone-core at Berth 9 cold storage. Beat four is collecting the contract pay at the payphone.\n\nThe heat bark is tagged to fire only when the heat meter reaches two or higher. No madness meter or its when-tags are introduced, since the quest must stay limited to payphone and heat.\n\nThe payoff call doubles as the second payphone to teach the router. The beat summary will carry the combat design. Beat 3 is planned as six waves of fifty-four weak human enemies with one-hit kills and no timer. Heat rises from one to three by wave four; ducking behind containers between waves drops it by one star. A searchlight boat at heat three adds pressure but is set dressing, not a new mechanic.\n\nThe payoff line will read exactly \"Cash 1800.\" The word \"soul\" is avoided entirely to prevent hints about soul 4 or Gearbox. The scientist's line about voices of the dead is kept vague; CORE-ZERO and core placement are never explained. The nurse's love hook is a threat: she will unstaple him if he calls it love, so no romance quest opens. The gunsmith's buddy hook names his brother-unit and mentions sit-privileges later, but he explicitly says they are not there.\n\nThe JSON payload is finalized as idempotent and valid. It uses pathId grok-A, seat grok, variant A, and act1. ClaimsBeat is omitted to avoid soul unlock claims. Story.writes will set q_berth9_sabotaged to true. The gunsmith quest flag is left out because the intro package alone is sufficient.\n\nThe gunsmith quest flag is dropped. Only q_berth9_sabotaged will be written to true. The unlock is set to weapon:loaner. The concern is whether giving a weapon counts as a new mechanic.\n\nThe loaner gun is a reward, not a new mechanic. The brief limits mechanics to the payphone router and heat. Factory combat was melee. The quest runs 12 minutes. Heat peaks at 3.\n\nThe drafted lines are being trimmed to roughly two sentences each, with one punchline per line. Rivet's finger-counting monologue is rewritten as a short clip about hands and suggestions. A static-bark line for Rivet is also drafted.\n\nPatch’s line is drafted as a job offer with a threat: answer the phone and a criminal wants you; walk away and Patch calls someone meaner. The fax-hire line is finalized, mocking Don Fax’s recycled precinct and demanding Berth Nine while warning that the dead hate the job.\n\nThe fax-hire line is tagged as funny, progression, and character. The \"the dead hate it\" remark is kept deliberately ambiguous so Don Fax cannot be read as knowing about the voices. Rivet's new line is drafted as a madcap monologue about bolts and becoming road infrastructure. A heat-bark line is also drafted for the payphone.\n\nThe fax heat line is finalized as a progression and funny clip about hiding behind crates to drop heat, with a boat at three stars. June Clamp’s healing line mocks zip-code leaks and stapling while warning that fainting costs extra. Her hook line threatens to pull the staple back out if he calls it love, forcing both to learn shame. Ned Rasp’s gun line opens with an ugly-resume comparison.\n\nNed Rasp’s gun line is finalized as a madcap clip about Loaner, the errand, and her hatred of heroes. His hook line names his brother-unit, warns that Loaner must return whole, and threatens to reveal his name only if the player sits. The scientist’s line is drafted as a mocking greeting to Lumen Peck, claiming he heard it from the stairs.\n\nLumen Peck’s greeting line is finalized as a mocking clip about agreeing to an un invented procedure. His hook line is drafted as a refusal to fix the player, demanding a whole verb from the dead before returning. The fax-brief line is drafted as a cold-storage mission: walk to Berth Nine, break four coupling pins, and throw the core into the slip.\n\nThe fax-brief line is finalized as a progression and funny clip about soldiers who die on a mean look, bullets that are memos, and a hatred of clocks. General Krump’s radio line is drafted as a madcap jab at Berth Nine readiness cradles and military budgets. Crumbtray’s line is started but left unfinished.\n\nCrumbtray’s line is finalized as a dock caller mocking Project STILL and telling his boys to cry on a form. Rivet’s pin-counting monologue is drafted as a madcap clip about cheering in his ear, throwing up in military style, and a horrible flute. A heat-bark line about hiding behind a box of war is also drafted.\n\nThe fax-pay line is finalized as a progression and character clip. It settles the 1800 cash and mocks finger-counting with a bulk-afternoon sales pitch. The rivet-after line is started but left unfinished.\n\nThe rivet-after line is finalized as a madcap clip about a crowd in his head and furious feet. Krump’s close line mocks the cradle going swimming and demands an industrial accident. The set now totals twenty lines with good range. The “we are not friends” phrasing is confirmed to pass the “we all” regex.\n\nThe \"that has always been enough\" line avoids the aphorism pattern. The \"clocks trust headquarters\" line is acceptable. June's hook uses a direct \"call it medicine\" structure rather than an \"it's not X\" setup. Ned's mention of Loaner as the gun is fine. The heat line caps at three stars and never introduces a fourth mechanic. Patch's car comment teaches the constraint in-world. Crumbtray must be listed as a character if he speaks. Speaker IDs are corrected: the nurse is not June, the gunsmith is not Ned, and the scientist is not Lumen.\n\nThe eight speakers are rivet, patch, don-fax, nurse, gunsmith, scientist, general-krump, and mc-crumbtray. All line IDs are assigned to beats. The ring beat covers the factory gate and payphone. The sink beat covers Carbon Sink introductions. The slip beat covers sabotage. The receipt beat covers the pay.\n\nThe JSON payload is being tightened to a single whyNotFiller string and a single goal string. Comments are removed. The brief’s profanity maximization is being applied selectively: rivet’s fingers line gains “Shit.”, the fax-hire line gets “Bullshit invoice”, June’s zip-code line uses “Goddamn zip code”, and Lumen Peck says “hell” clinically. Ned’s “Shoot, you nervous fuck” is dropped as redundant.\n\nRivet’s fingers line opens with “Shit.” The fax-brief line mocks bullets as a “shitty memo.” Rivet’s pin-counting clip adds “Fuck” at the heat peak. Don stays clipped and Krump stays clean. The contrast makes Krump creepier and the jokes hit harder.\n\nRivet’s lines are deliberately unstable. Peck is the mad-scientist and Don is the controlled one, which amplifies Rivet’s lunacy.\n\nThe grok take differs from a generic warehouse tour. Project STILL is a kneeling-tone rather than a bomb. Sabotage uses four coupling pins and a splash. The three hooks are physical and specific: a staple frequency, a gun with a dead brother’s name withheld, and a verb in the static. Each character refuses the relationship today.\n\nHeat is framed as coward-professionalism capped at three stars. Patch is a judgmental operator who teaches the payphone rule twice, and the beat summary will note that Don is already on the phone when it rings. A new Patch line was considered but rejected to avoid crowding the twenty-line set; fax-pay opens with Don instead.\n\nThe introduction order is now fixed: June Clamp first, then Ned Rasp, then Lumen Peck, then the fax-brief. The Carbon Sink is underground beneath a closed customs scale. The walk to Berth Nine is on foot; phones along the way are inert until one rings. JSON quotes will be escaped.\n\nLine IDs and character IDs match the required regex. The JSON will omit claimsBeat entirely, set audio to null on every line, and keep purpose tags unique within each array. Mechanic home is game, with payphone-job-router and heat as the two mechanics. Beat summaries must be at least one character long and specific enough to build from.\n\nThe payphone summary now specifies that answering locks the job while walking away sends it to another robot, and the payoff rings back on the nearest phone without a menu. The heat summary frames the star meter as rising when army witnesses see Rivet fight and falling behind crates, with a searchlight boat joining at three stars; humans die in one hit and their bullets are weak, so waves are the real threat. The searchlight boat is confirmed as part of the heat mechanic rather than a separate system. Three fail states satisfy the minimum requirement.\n\nThe wave breakdown totals fifty-four enemies. Hiding between waves reduces heat but does not skip scripted spawns at the sabotage pins. Heat changes density and the searchlight boat, yet it never halts the quest. The pin channel has no timer; each pin is a short interruptible interaction, so timeLimitS is null.\n\nThe \"it's not X\" regex is confirmed safe from false matches like \"That is not a riddle.\" Only contractions such as \"that's\" trigger it. Plain ASCII punctuation will be used throughout.\n\nThe rivet-after line is revised to \"In the water you go, you horrible flute.\" The whyNotFiller will be sharpened so each laugh is unmistakably a filler. The title is finalized as \"Four Pins at Berth Nine.\"\n\nThe title is finalized as \"Four Pins\". The logline will be one sentence. The beat structure now opens at the factory gate immediately after Rivet survives the line accident and disarms the police, then passes through Don's work, the three hooks, Krump's notice, and ends on the street with Rivet on foot as heat cools. The owner beat is confirmed: Don Fax hires Rivet.\n\nThe quest is the first boss, so no second boss is needed. Lady Cathode and other characters will be omitted. The 1800 cash and the line match. The weapon hook unlocks a persistent loaner gun, which the gunsmith hook brings back. The story variable is renamed to q_berth_nine_sabotaged for clarity.\n\nThe new story variable is a boolean flag requiring writes and carrying no reads. June Clamp’s name fits the staple motif, Ned Rasp’s name fits the gunsmith role, and Lumen Peck’s name evokes light and the bird-doctor.\n\nLumen Peck is fast, happy, and dangerous with curiosity. He is not a gentle mentor yet.\n\nNed Rasp treats the gun as a person. He is gruff and superstitious. He withholds friendship.\n\nJune Clamp uses triage humor. She is ashamed of tenderness. She threatens to undo her hook. She is tired.\n\nDon Fax is a harbor clerk of murder. He never raises his voice. He counts stars. He hates clocks and bad invoices.\n\nRivet restarts and counts. He panics profanely. He hears noise he cannot parse.\n\nGeneral Krump reads sponsor files. He speaks with clean diction. He despises feelings and appliances.\n\nCrumbtray is an auctioneer with one army joke and Patch is a bored literal operator. Both are distinct. The twenty-line count is valid. One-line design notes will be added. Roles are assigned: Crumbtray and Patch as voice, Krump as antagonist, Don as giver, and the three as allies. Rivet is being reconsidered because the player cannot be his own ally.\n\nRivet is assigned