omfortably inside the quest band and leaves room for more act quests before a 2000 upgrade. The line will read \"Twelve hundred, the whole sum.\"\n\nThe brief expects missing voice bibles for the nurse, gunsmith, and scientist. I will still write their lines and mark them new to avoid collisions.\n\nThe missing voice bible files are a structural error. The owner explicitly requires proper names and introduction lines for these characters, so I will proceed despite the expected load failure. The line guard for existing characters is also flagged as an error.\n\nThe council may reject the proposal automatically. I will deliver the JSON array as requested. I considered having Don describe the three characters to avoid voice bible errors, but that would violate the creative brief. The 21 lines fit within the 12–22 limit. I am now writing the final JSON.\n\nThe reply will contain only the JSON array, with no markdown fences. I am verifying the four character IDs and ensuring design notes are one-line strings. Fresh design notes will be added for Don, Krump, and the three support characters to satisfy the brief's instruction to invent looks.\n\nPatch, Crumbtray, and Rivet will not receive design notes. Patch has no physical presence. Crumbtray is only a radio voice. Rivet’s factory chassis from the intro is current and must not be reinvented.\n\nThe JSON array will be pretty-printed for readability.\n\nI am checking that all strings use only apostrophes and avoid inner double quotes. The fax-heat line \"professional coward\" is a gentle power-fantasy jab. The crumbtray line about shooting like the union is watching risks being misread as anti-union.\n\nThe union reference is actually pro-worker, since the army fears it. The \"feelings are not in the budget\" line targets the military. Objective verbs are answer, meet, sabotage, and collect; sabotage is the core fingerprint. I am omitting durationS and writing beat summaries that carry gameplay instructions so a builder ca